s False on macOS). On that POSIX host the test takes the Windows branch, the 0644 key composes, and the test stays green while the 0600 refusal is gone."],"finding":"The new platform-rule test decides which outcome to expect from the production gate itself (keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS), not from an independent platform check. The test cannot see a regression in that gate: if _POSIX_MODE_BITS evaluates False on Linux or macOS, the 0600 check is silently dropped there. The test then takes the Windows branch, the 0644 key composes, `assert binding is not None` holds, and the test passes. It also ties the test to a private name, so renaming the constant breaks it even when behavior is unchanged.","id":"0","impact":0.24,"location":"tests/unit/test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py:476","norm_id":"n1e87852c3286e745","priority":0.1029,"reason":"low-validity","reviewer_id":"code-review-tests","reviewers":["code-review-tests"],"scenarios":["A refactor of keyprov's platform detection wrongly disables the 0600 check on POSIX. This test, the only one pinning the group-readable rule per platform, still passes.","Renaming or inlining the private _POSIX_MODE_BITS constant, with no behavior change, makes the test fail with AttributeError."],"suggested_fix":"Replace `if keyprov._POSIX_MODE_BITS:` with an independent check such as `if os.name != \"nt\":` (or `sys.platform != \"win32\"`). Then drop the `from rebar.opcert_service import keyprov` import if nothing else uses it.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4286,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","respects_artifact_altitude":"na","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"blast_radius":"local